# Generated from: specs/13-protect-live-workflow-state-during-coordinator-exceptions/requirements.md
Feature: Protect live workflow state during coordinator exceptions
  As a volunteer coordinator
  I want deactivation and assignment correction to resolve every related workflow record atomically
  So that the schedule never hides commitments that volunteers can still act upon

  @SCN001
  Scenario: Deactivation resolves all live workflow records
    Given an active shift has a pending request, an assigned volunteer, a confirmed volunteer, and unused assignment action tokens
    And an allowlisted coordinator has the shift's current version
    And the schedule page explains that deactivation will supersede pending requests, cancel active assignments, and invalidate their action links
    When the coordinator deactivates the shift
    Then the shift is inactive
    And every pending request for its slots is superseded by that coordinator
    And every active assignment for its slots is cancelled
    And every unused action token for those assignments is unusable
    And the complete transition and coordinator audits commit atomically
    And the shift is absent from public openings and coverage without hidden live workflow state

  @SCN002
  Scenario: Coordinator cancels an active assignment
    Given a published slot has a confirmed assignment with unused action tokens
    And an allowlisted coordinator is authenticated
    When the coordinator cancels the assignment from coverage
    Then the assignment is cancelled
    And the slot is reported as Uncovered
    And every previously issued action link fails without mutation
    And AssignmentCancelledByCoordinator identifies the coordinator in the audit trail

  @SCN003
  Scenario Outline: Requests and coverage agree on active assignment state
    Given a request targets a published future slot whose active assignment is <assignment_status>
    When an allowlisted coordinator views requests and coverage
    Then the request queue reports the slot as <coordinator_state>
    And coverage reports the slot as <coordinator_state>
    And the request cannot be approved as an available slot

    Examples:
      | assignment_status | coordinator_state |
      | Assigned          | Unconfirmed       |
      | Confirmed         | Confirmed         |

  @SCN004
  Scenario: Conflicting correction transitions leave one authoritative state
    Given separate PostgreSQL transactions target the same slot with assignment, action-link generation, volunteer-action, coordinator-cancellation, or shift-deactivation transitions
    When the transactions attempt to commit
    Then slot locking serializes commands that create, administratively end, act on, or generate links for assignments
    And concurrency tokens or active-state uniqueness reject stale conflicting writes
    And the committed state is one valid lifecycle outcome
    And an inactive shift has no pending request, active assignment, or usable assignment action token

  @SCN005
  Scenario: Coordinator reassignment invalidates superseded assignment links
    Given a slot has an active assignment with unused action tokens
    And an allowlisted coordinator is authenticated
    When the coordinator reassigns the slot to another volunteer
    Then the previous assignment is Reassigned
    And every action link issued for the previous assignment fails without mutation
    And the replacement assignment remains the only active assignment for the slot

